<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cam Ranh Bay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cam Ranh Bay is a deep natural harbour in Khánh Hòa province on the south-central coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>. The Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-fleet">Baltic Fleet</a> anchored there in 1905 on its way to Tsushima, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> built a large base there during the Vietnam War, and from 1979 to 2002 it was the main Soviet and then Russian naval base abroad. It remains an important Vietnamese naval base.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cam-ranh-bay.jpg" alt="Cam Ranh Bay" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cam_Ranh_Bay_Landsat_elevation_image.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cam Ranh Bay is an inlet of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-china-sea">South China Sea</a> between Phan Rang and Nha Trang, about 290 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ho-chi-minh-city">Ho Chi Minh City</a>. The continental shelf is narrow there, bringing deep water close to land, and the bay is considered the finest deep-water shelter in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>. The French used it as a naval base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a>, and in 1942 the Japanese Imperial Navy prepared there for the invasion of British Malaya and British Borneo. In January 1945 United States Task Force 38 destroyed most of the Japanese facilities, and the bay was abandoned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After a North Vietnamese trawler was found landing munitions at nearby Vũng Rô Bay in February 1965, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> made Cam Ranh a major base. Army engineers built roads and lengthened the pier, and the consortium RMK-BRJ, employing 1,800 Vietnamese workers, over half of them women, laid a 10,000-foot runway of aluminium matting in 50 days, completed on 16 October 1965. The air base was one of three ports through which American soldiers entered or left South Vietnam, and the Navy based its coastal patrol aircraft there. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-cong">Viet Cong</a> sapper attack on 7 August 1969 killed two Americans at the 6th Convalescent Center.</p><p>The facilities passed to South Vietnam in 1972, and North Vietnamese forces captured the bay on 3 April 1975. In 1979 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> signed a 25-year lease, and the base grew to four times its original size by 1987. Reductions began by 1990, and the base reverted to sole Vietnamese control on 2 May 2002, when the Russian flag was lowered for the last time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cam Ranh is a major base of the Vietnam People's Navy, the headquarters of its 4th Regional Command and of the Vietnam Naval Air Force, and home to the country's only submarine base. In October 2010 Prime Minister Nguyen Tan Dung announced that it would reopen to foreign navies, and in October 2016 two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">United States Navy</a> ships made the first American port visit since 1975. Ba Ngoi Port, within the bay, is an international commercial port.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cam Ranh Bay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its alliance with the Soviet Union, sealed by a treaty of friendship in November 1978 and membership of Comecon, gave Moscow use of the former American base at <b>Cam Ranh Bay</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-republic-of-vietnam">Socialist Republic of Vietnam · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
